# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local tooling. Every contributor uses different editors and helpers, and none
# of that configuration belongs to anyone else. Dot directories at the root are
# ignored by default; the two that are genuinely part of the project are let
# back in explicitly.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/.*/
!/.cargo/
!/.github/
!/.githooks/
*.code-workspace

# Personal working notes, kept next to the code but never published.
/private/
*.private.md
*.local.md
*.local.toml
*.local.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secrets and credentials. Nothing here ever reaches the repository.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.mobileprovision
*.p8
*.cer
*.certSigningRequest
*.keystore
*.jks
secrets/
*.pairing
*.plist.local

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rust
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/target/
**/target/
*.pdb
*.rs.bk
# Cargo.lock IS committed: builds must be reproducible (--locked).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Node / frontend
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
node_modules/
dist/
dist-ssr/
# The preview build, which must never be mistaken for the real bundle and must never be
# published anywhere.
dist-preview/
*.tsbuildinfo

# What the accessibility gate leaves behind: a report, and a trace for anything that
# failed. Both are rebuilt by every run and neither belongs in the history.
test-results/
playwright-report/
npm-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*
# package-lock.json IS committed.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tauri / mobile build output
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
src-tauri/target/
src-tauri/gen/schemas/
gen/apple/build/
gen/apple/*.xcworkspace/xcuserdata/
gen/apple/*.xcodeproj/xcuserdata/
gen/apple/Externals/
DerivedData/
*.ipa
*.dSYM.zip
*.xcarchive

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Application data. Real databases and backups must never be committed.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
*.cairn
*.cairnbackup
backups/

# Two exceptions, and both are deliberate. The frozen backup fixtures are the only proof that
# a file written by an older build still opens, so they have to be in the repository. They hold
# six invented rows and a password that is printed in the test beside them.
!crates/cairn-db/tests/fixtures/*.cairn
# The fuzzing seed is a copy of that same file and holds nothing else. A fuzzer handed no
# example of a real backup spends its whole run discovering the eight bytes of magic.
!fuzz/corpus/export_stream/*.cairn

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Test, coverage and audit artifacts
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
coverage/
lcov.info
*.profraw
*.profdata
fuzz/artifacts/
fuzz/coverage/
# Every corpus except two, and from those two only the seeds. A fuzzer that starts from nothing
# spends its first minutes rediscovering that a record is a line of JSON and that a backup
# begins with eight bytes of magic, and a nightly run is too short to afford that. What is
# committed is a handful of hand-written inputs and one real backup; everything a run finds for
# itself stays out of the repository and is carried between runs by the cache.
#
# The directories are named rather than the files, because git does not look inside a directory
# it has been told to ignore: `fuzz/corpus/` as a single line would make every negation below
# it dead text. And each directory's contents are then ignored again, because un-ignoring the
# directory un-ignores everything in it — a single minute of fuzzing leaves several thousand
# files there, and they would all have turned up as untracked.
#
# The seeds are told apart from the findings by having an extension. libFuzzer names what it
# writes after the SHA-1 of its contents and gives it no suffix, so this is a rule the fuzzer
# cannot accidentally satisfy.
fuzz/corpus/*
!fuzz/corpus/import_parser/
fuzz/corpus/import_parser/*
!fuzz/corpus/import_parser/*.jsonl
!fuzz/corpus/export_stream/
fuzz/corpus/export_stream/*
!fuzz/corpus/export_stream/*.cairn
mutants.out*/
criterion/
audit-report*.json
sbom*.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OS noise
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Thumbs.db
ehthumbs.db
Desktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/
.DS_Store
._*
*~
